{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TrZH3WCXUUPXeC85zqA3qy","type":"tool_result","content":"7109\t### Part 4-3. Modal Dependency\n7110\t\n7111\tUMR represents modal strength and polarity as a dependency structure.\n7112\tThe nodes are either events or conceivers (i.e., a source, an entity\n7113\twhose perspective on an event is modelled in the text). The edges in the dependency structure correspond to modal strength and polarity values (i.e., how certain a specific conceiver is about the occurrence of the event in the real world). Annotators do not have to construct the dependency structure directly, but it can be built up “behind the\n7114\tscenes” by annotating some modal/polarity information and leveraging\n7115\tthe participant role annotation.\n7116\t\n7117\tThe modal annotation captures the modal strength of events, but not the modal type of the event (i.e., epistemic/evidential, deontic,\n7118\tpermissive, etc.). These modal strengths are annotated based on a\n7119\tlattice of annotation values that differ in terms of granularity. An\n7120\texample of the UMR annotation and the underlying modal dependency\n7121\tstructure is shown below in <a href=\"#4-3 (1)\">(1)</a>.\n7122\t\n7123\t<span id=\"4-3 (1)\" label=\"4-3 (1)\">4-3 (1)</span>\n7124\t\n7125\t![Modal dependency structure](https://github.com/umr4nlp/umr-guidelines/blob/master/Guidelines_figures/Modal%20Dep_2022.jpg)\n7126\t\n7127\t```\n7128\tMartin said that the package has probably already arrived.\n7129\t(s/ say-01  \n7130\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7131\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Martin\")\n7132\t\t:ARG1 (a/ arrive-01  \n7133\t\t\t:ARG1 (p/ package\n7134\t\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)  \n7135\t\t\tquot: s\n7136\t\t\tmodstr: PrtAff)\n7137\t\tmodstr: Aff)\n7138\t(s/ sentence\n7139\t\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1s)\n7140\t\t\t   (s1s :before s1a))\n7141\t\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1p)\n7142\t\t\t(AUTH :FullAff s1s)\n7143\t\t\t(s1p :PrtAff s1a)))\n7144\t```\n7145\t\n7146\tThe *say*-event and the *arrive*-event each get a modal strength value (`:modstr`), which can then be represented\n7147\tin the dependency as an edge between the event and the relevant conceiver/source. The\n7148\t`:quot` value indicates than an event is\n7149\tbeing reported, and the participant role annotation can be used to\n7150\tautomatically select the conceiver for the reported event(s), here,\n7151\t*Martin*.\n7152\t\n7153\tThis simplified, mostly sentence-level, annotation process is step 0 of the road map for modal annotation. Step 1 builds on this step to end up at a fully specified modal dependency structure. This means that Stage 1 annotation involves first doing the Stage 0 modality annotations. (This contrasts with participant roles, where annotators can largely ignore Stage 0 if the annotation language has existing frame files.)\n7154\t\n7155\t[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n7156\t\n7157\t#### Part 4-3-1. Stage 0\n7158\t\n7159\tThere are two types of modal annotations at Stage 0: a\n7160\t`:modstr` annotation that consists of a\n7161\tsingle epistemic strength/polarity value, and a dependency annotation\n7162\tthat indicates a relation between two events. There are two dependency\n7163\trelations: `:modpred` for the link between a\n7164\tmodal event and the event(s) that it modalizes, and\n7165\t`:quot` for the link between a reporting\n7166\tevent and the event(s) that it reports. Additionally, events in purpose clauses and events in conditional constructions must be taken up in the modal dependency tree. The right structure of the modal dependency graph for these events will be extrapolated from the sentence-level annotation: events in purpose clauses are daughters of events in the main clause they depend on, and are connected to them with the `:purpose` participant role. This can be leveraged to embed the purpose-event underneath the correct parent in the modal dependency graph as well. For conditionals, similarly, the protasis event will be embedded under the apodosis event with a `:condition` relation, or the protasis and apodosis events will both be ```:ARG1``` and ```:ARG2``` of a `have-condition-91` node. Again, this information will be taken up in the eventual modal dependency graph without annotators needing to indicate it a second time in the document-level modal annotation workflow.\n7167\tThe `:modstr` annotation applies to all events, except for those under the scope of a modal identified as its own event (i.e., events with `:mod` relations). This\n7168\tis summarized below.\n7169\t\n7170\t<div id=\"tab:modal_relations\">\n7171\t\n7172\t| **Event type** | **UMR Treatment** |\n7173\t| :------------------------------- |:------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n7174\t| Events under the scope of modals | `:modpred` relation to CPT |\n7175\t| Events under the scope of reporting events| `:modstr` annotation, `:quot` relation to reporting event |\n7176\t| All other events | `:modstr` annotation |\n7177\t\n7178\tTable 14: Modal relations at Stage 0\n7179\t</div>\n7180\t\n7181\t[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n7182\t\n7183\t##### Part 4-3-1-1. modstr values\n7184\t\n7185\tThe modal strength values correspond to epistemic strength, i.e. the\n7186\tauthor or conceiver’s certainty about the occurrence of the event in the real world, or certainty about another conceiver’s mental content. Based on Boye (2012), a typological study of modal systems across languages, and following FactBank (Pustejovsky et al. 2005), the UMR annotation is based around three levels of modal strength: `Full`, `Partial`, and `Neutral`, illustrated in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1 (1)\">(1)</a>.\n7187\t\n7188\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-1 (1)\">4-3-1-1 (1)</span>\n7189\t\n7190\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1 (1a)\" label=\"4-3-1-1 (1a)\">4-3-1-1 (1a)</span> Full:  \n7191\tThe cat already **ate** breakfast.\n7192\t\n7193\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1 (1b)\" label=\"4-3-1-1 (1b)\">4-3-1-1 (1b)</span>\n7194\tPartial:  \n7195\tThe cat <u>probably</u> already **ate** breakfast.  \n7196\t\n7197\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1 (1c)\" label=\"4-3-1-1 (1c)\">4-3-1-1 (1c)</span>\n7198\tNeutral:  \n7199\tThe cat <u>might</u> have already **eaten** breakfast.\n7200\t\n7201\tThe `Full` modal strength value, as in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1 (1a)\">(1a)</a>, corresponds to complete certainty; that is, the conceiver is 100% certain that the event occurs in the real world. The `Neutral` modal strength value, shown in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1 (1c)\">(1c)</a>, indicates the possibility of the event; essentially, this corresponds to 50/50 certainty that the event occurs in the real world. The `Partial` modal strength value, as in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1 (1b)\">(1b)</a>, falls between the `Full` and `Neutral` values; the conceiver believes that more likely than not, the event occurs in the real world.\n7202\t\n7203\tBut, `Full`, `Partial`, and `Neutral` aren’t the only possible modal strength annotation values. Languages differ in the modal strength distinctions that are conventionalized in their grammar. In order to accommodate these differences, we use a typological lattice of annotation values, constructed based on the structure of the annotation category across languages (Van Gysel et al. 2019).\n7204\t\n7205\tOne level of granularity in the lattice is designated as the “base\n7206\tlevel”: annotators are encouraged to use categories from this level as\n7207\tthe default. These values are selected as the base level because these\n7208\tdistinctions occur the most frequently across languages. The higher and lower levels, respectively, contain equally typologically-motivated coarser-grained and finer-grained categories, which can be used when a language conventionalizes these distinctions in its grammar. Such lattices capture the idea that many semantic categories are structured as hierarchical scales, where the middle values can group together with either end, but the extremes of the scale are highly unlikely to be categorized together in any language. For example, no language has a grammatical form that is used for both `Full` and `Neutral` epistemic strength, but not `Partial`. The typological lattice for epistemic strength is shown below.\n7209\t\n7210\t![Modal strength lattice](https://github.com/umr4nlp/umr-guidelines/blob/master/Guidelines_figures/Affirmative%20Modal%20Strength%20Lattice_2022.png)\n7211\t\n7212\t![Modal strength lattice](https://github.com/umr4nlp/umr-guidelines/blob/master/Guidelines_figures/Negative%20Modal%20Strength%20Lattice_2022.png)\n7213\t\n7214\tThis lattice is based around the base level of `Full` vs. `Partial` vs.\n7215\t`Neutral`, but also allows for the annotation of more coarse-grained\n7216\tvalues that lump together the distinctions in the base level, and more\n7217\tfine-grained annotation values. For contexts where it is unclear if the modal strength is `Full` or `Partial`, the `Non-neutral` value can be used; if it is unclear whether the modal strength is `Partial` or `Neutral`, then the `Non-full` value can be used. The most fine-grained modal strength values are generally used with languages that have grammatical forms that encode the relevant distinction.\n7218\t\n7219\tAlso following FactBank (Pustejovsky et al. 2006), the `:modstr`\n7220\tannotation combines the epistemic strength values with a binary polarity distinction (`Affirmative`, `Negative`). This results in six modal strength/polarity values for the default level, shown below. Both the affirmative values and the negative values have their own set of related coarse-grained and fine-grained values.\n7221\t\n7222\t<div>\n7223\t\n7224\t| **Label**                              | **Value**                              |\n7225\t| :------------------------------------- | :------------------------------------- |\n7226\t| ```FullAff```     | full strength, affirmative polarity    |\n7227\t| ```PrtAff```     | partial strength, affirmative polarity |\n7228\t| ```NeutAFf```    | neutral strength, affirmative polarity |\n7229\t| ```NeutNeg``` | neutral strength, negative polarity    |\n7230\t| ```PrtNeg```  | partial strength, negative polarity    |\n7231\t| ```FullNeg```    | full strength, negative polarity       |\n7232\t\n7233\t</div>\n7234\t\n7235\tThese values and their interpretation are shown below; the corresponding FactBank values are in parentheses.\n7236\t\n7237\t```FullAff```: full affirmative support; complete certainty that the event occurs (CT+)  \n7238\t```PrtAff```: partial affirmative support; there is strong, but not definitive certainty that the event occurs (PR+)  \n7239\t```NeutAff```: affirmative neutral support; there is neutral certainty that the event occurs/doesn’t occur; event is expressed positively (PS+)  \n7240\t```NeutNeg```: negative neutral support; there is neutral certainty that the event occurs/doesn’t occur; negation of event is expressed (PS-)  \n7241\t```PrtNeg```: partial negative support; there is strong but not definitive certainty that the event does not occur (PR-)  \n7242\t```FullNeg```: full negative support; complete certainty that the event does not occur (CT-)\n7243\t\n7244\tDegree of certainty corresponds most straightforwardly to the degree of confidence of a conceiver (often, the author) in the occurrence of an episodic event, i.e. the epistemic continuum from certainty to\n7245\tpossibility. We use these same values for the evidential continuum from direct evidence to second-hand (reported or inferred) evidence; see [Part 4-3-1-1-2](#part-4-3-1-1-2-evidential-justification) below). And these values are\n7246\tinterpreted into the domain of future-oriented or deontic modality, as\n7247\texplained in [Part 4-3-1-1-3](#part-4-3-1-1-3-future-events-and-deontic-modality). The interpretation of\n7248\tthe value - as epistemic, evidential or deontic - is not reflected in\n7249\tthe modal strength annotation.\n7250\t\n7251\t[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n7252\t\n7253\t##### Part 4-3-1-1-1. Non-future events\n7254\t\n7255\tFor non-future (non-deontic) events, the `modstr` values correspond to the author’s level of certainty towards the occurrence of the event in the real world. Events presented as fact are annotated with\n7256\t`FullAff`, while events for which the author categorically denies their occurrence are annotated `FullNeg`. When the author doesn’t present the\n7257\tevent as fact, but has a higher level of certainty towards the event\n7258\teither being true or not true, this is annotated as\n7259\t`PrtAff` or, when the polarity is negative, `PrtNeg`. When the author doesn’t lean either direction towards the event being true in the real world or not, the event is annotated as `NeutAff` or\n7260\t`NeutNeg`, depending on the polarity of the\n7261\tlinguistic expression. These strength values are exemplified in\n7262\t<a href=\"#4-3-1-1-1 (1)\">(1)</a>.\n7263\t\n7264\t\n7265\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1)\">4-3-1-1-1 (1)</span>\n7266\t\n7267\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1a)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1a)\">4-3-1-1-1 (1a)</span>   \n7268\t```\n7269\tThe dog barked last night.\n7270\t(b/ bark-01\n7271\t\t:ARG0 (d/ dog\n7272\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7273\t\t:temporal (n/ night\n7274\t\t\t:mod (l/ last))\n7275\t\t:aspect Endeavor\n7276\t\t:modstr FullAff)  \n7277\t```\n7278\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1b)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1b)\">4-3-1-1-1 (1b)</span>  \n7279\t```\n7280\tThe dog probably barked last night.\n7281\t(b/ bark-01\n7282\t\t:ARG0 (d/ dog\n7283\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7284\t\t:temporal (n/ night\n7285\t\t\t:mod (l/ last))\n7286\t\t:aspect Endeavor\n7287\t\t:modstr PrtAff)  \n7288\t```\n7289\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1c)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1c)\">4-3-1-1-1 (1c)</span>\n7290\t```\n7291\tThe dog may have barked last night.\n7292\t(b/ bark-01\n7293\t\t:ARG0 (d/ dog\n7294\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7295\t\t:temporal (n/ night\n7296\t\t\t:mod (l/ last))\n7297\t\t:aspect Endeavor\n7298\t\t:modstr NeutAff)  \n7299\t```\n7300\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1d)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1d)\">4-3-1-1-1 (1d)</span> \n7301\t```\n7302\tThe dog may not have barked last night.\n7303\t(b/ bark-01\n7304\t\t:ARG0 (d/ dog\n7305\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7306\t\t:temporal (n/ night\n7307\t\t\t:mod (l/ last))\n7308\t\t:aspect Endeavor\n7309\t\t:modstr NeutNeg)  \n7310\t```\n7311\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1e)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1e)\">4-3-1-1-1 (1e)</span>\n7312\t```\n7313\tThe dog probably didn't bark last night.\n7314\t(b/ bark-01\n7315\t\t:ARG0 (d/ dog\n7316\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7317\t\t:temporal (n/ night\n7318\t\t\t:mod (l/ last))\n7319\t\t:aspect Endeavor\n7320\t\t:modstr PrtNeg)  \n7321\t``` \n7322\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1f)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1f)\">4-3-1-1-1 (1f)</span>\n7323\t```   \n7324\tThe dog didn't bark last night.\n7325\t(b/ bark-01\n7326\t\t:ARG0 (d/ dog\n7327\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7328\t\t:temporal (n/ night\n7329\t\t\t:mod (l/ last))\n7330\t\t:aspect Endeavor\n7331\t\t:modstr FullNeg)  \n7332\t```  \n7333\t[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n7334\t\n7335\t##### Part 4-3-1-1-2. Evidential justification\n7336\t\n7337\tFollowing Boye (2012) and Saurí and Pustejovsky (2009), we conflate evidential justification with epistemic support. Boye (2012) finds that there is cross-linguistic evidence for lumping epistemic support and evidential justification together into the same relations. Languages may encode direct evidential justification (sensory perception) with the same forms as full epistemic support; indirect justification (hearsay, inferential) may be encoded by the same forms as partial epistemic support.\n7338\t\n7339\tExample <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-2 (1)\">(1)</a> shows how direct and indirect justification correspond to epistemic support.\n7340\t\n7341\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1-2 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-2 (1)\">4-3-1-1-2 (1)</span>\n7342\t\n7343\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1-2 (1a)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-2 (1a)\">4-3-1-1-2 (1a)</span> \n7344\t```\n7345\t(I saw) Mary feed the cat.\n7346\t(s/ see-01\n7347\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7348\t\t\t:ref-person 1st\n7349\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7350\t\t:ARG1 (f/ feed-01\n7351\t\t\t:ARG0 (p2/ person\n7352\t\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n7353\t\t\t:ARG2 (c/ cat\n7354\t\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7355\t\t\t:aspect Performance\n7356\t\t\t**:modstr FullAff**)\n7357\t\t:aspect State\n7358\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n7359\t```\n7360\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1-2 (1b)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-2 (1b)\">4-3-1-1-2 (1b)</span> \n7361\t```\n7362\tMary must have fed the cat.\n7363\t(f/ feed-01\n7364\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7365\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n7366\t\t:ARG2 (c/ cat\n7367\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7368\t\t:aspect Performance\n7369\t\t:modstr PrtAff)\n7370\t```\n7371\t\n7372\tIn <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-2 (1a)\">(1a)</a>, the author has direct knowledge of the feeding event, by way of witnessing it. Therefore, *feed* is annotated with `Aff` modal strength. In <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-2 (1b)\">(1b)</a>, however, *must* signals that the author is inferring that the feeding event occurred without direct, perceptual knowledge. Therefore, *fed* in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-2 (1b)\">(1b)</a> is annotated with `Prt` modal strength.\n7373\t\n7374\t[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n7375\t\n7376\t##### Part 4-3-1-1-3. Future events and deontic modality\n7377\t\n7378\tFor events presented as (potentially) happening in the future,\n7379\t`:modstr` refers to the predictability of\n7380\tthe occurrence of the event in the future, as presented by the author.\n7381\tPredictive future has full strength (`FullAff`\n7382\tor `FullNeg`); intentions and commands\n7383\tcorrespond to partial strength (`PrtAff` or\n7384\t`PrtNeg`); and desire and permission\n7385\tcorrespond to neutral (`NeutAff` or\n7386\t`NeutNeg`) strength. Keep in mind that events under the scope of modals identified as their own event don't receive any `:modstr` value at all. This section refers to deontic meanings indicated by grammaticalized modals that don't fit the criteria to be identified as events.\n7387\t\n7388\tThis is illustrated in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-3 (1)\">(1)</a>.\n7389\t\n7390\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1)\">4-3-1-1-3 (1)</span>\n7391\t\n7392\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1a)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1a)\">4-3-1-1-3 (1a)</span>  \n7393\t```\n7394\tI will go to Santa Fe.\n7395\t(g/ go-01\n7396\t\t:ARG1 (p/ person\n7397\t\t\t:ref-person 1st\n7398\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7399\t\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n7400\t\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n7401\t\t\t:name (n/ name\n7402\t\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n7403\t\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe\"))\n7404\t\t:aspect Performance\n7405\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n7406\t```\n7407\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1b)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1b)\">4-3-1-1-3 (1b)</span>   \n7408\t```\n7409\tYou must go to Santa Fe.\n7410\t(g/ go-01\n7411\t\t:ARG1 (p/ person\n7412\t\t\t:ref-person 2nd\n7413\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7414\t\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n7415\t\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n7416\t\t\t:name (n/ name\n7417\t\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n7418\t\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe\"))\n7419\t\t:aspect Performance\n7420\t\t:modstr PrtAff\n7421\t\t:mode Imperative)\n7422\t```\n7423\t\n7424\t<span id=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1c)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1c)\">4-3-1-1-3 (1c)</span>   \n7425\t```\n7426\tYou can go to Santa Fe.\n7427\t(g/ go-01\n7428\t\t:ARG1 (p/ person\n7429\t\t\t:ref-person 2nd\n7430\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7431\t\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n7432\t\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n7433\t\t\t:name (n/ name\n7434\t\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n7435\t\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe\"))\n7436\t\t:aspect Performance\n7437\t\t:modstr NeutAff)\n7438\t```\n7439\tThe predictive future, as in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-3 (1a)\">(1a)</a>, is annotated with\n7440\tfull modal strength because it presents the future event as a certainty\n7441\t(i.e., it is as certain as is possible for future events). Commands, as\n7442\tin <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-3 (1b)\">(1b)</a>, are annotated with partial modal strength\n7443\tbecause they present the future event as less likely than the predictive\n7444\tfuture, but more likely to happen than the neutral strength deontics.\n7445\tFinally, permission, as in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-3 (1c)\">(1c)</a>, is annotated as\n7446\tneutral strength, since even if someone has permission to do something, there is no guarantee they will.\n7447\t\n7448\t[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n7449\t\n7450\t##### Part 4-3-1-2. modpred relation\n7451\t\n7452\tEvents under the scope of a modal identified as its own event are only\n7453\tannotated with a `:modpred` relation to the\n7454\trelevant modal. This is shown below in <a href=\"#4-3-1-2 (1)\">(1)</a>.\n7455\t\n7456\t<span id=\"4-3-1-2 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-2 (1)\">4-3-1-2 (1)</span>\n7457\t\n7458\t<span id=\"4-3-1-2 (1a)\" label=\"4-3-1-2 (1a)\">4-3-1-2 (1a)</span> \n7459\t```\n7460\tMary wants to visit France.\n7461\t(w/ want-01\n7462\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7463\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n7464\t\t:ARG1 (v/ visit-01\n7465\t\t\t:ARG0 p\n7466\t\t\t:ARG1 (c/ country\n7467\t\t\t\t:wiki \"France\"\n7468\t\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"France\"))\n7469\t\t\t:aspect Performance\n7470\t\t\t:modpred w\n7471\t\t:aspect State\n7472\t\t:modstr FullAff\n7473\t\n7474\t(s/ sentence\n7475\t\t:temporal ((DCT :overlap s1w)\n7476\t\t\t   (s1w :after s1v))\n7477\t\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1p)\n7478\t\t\t(s1p :FullAff s1w)\n7479\t\t\t(s1w :Unsp s1v))\n7480\t```\n7481\t<span id=\"4-3-1-2 (1b)\" label=\"4-3-1-2 (1b)\">4-3-1-2 (1b)</span> \n7482\t```\n7483\tRob thinks the dog escaped through the fence.\n7484\t(t/ think-01\n7485\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7486\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Rob\"))\n7487\t\t:ARG1 (e/ escape-01\n7488\t\t\t:ARG0 (d/ dog\n7489\t\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7490\t\t\t:path (f/ fence)\n7491\t\t\t:aspect Performance\n7492\t\t\t:modpred t)\n7493\t\t:aspect State\n7494\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n7495\t(s/ sentence\n7496\t\t:temporal ((DCT :overlap s1t)\n7497\t\t\t   (s1t :before s1e))\n7498\t\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1p)\n7499\t\t\t(s1p :FullAff s1t)\n7500\t\t\t(s1t :Unsp s1e)))\n7501\t```\n7502\t<span id=\"4-3-1-2 (1c)\" label=\"4-3-1-2 (1c)\">4-3-1-2 (1c)</span> \n7503\t```\n7504\tThey probably decided to leave on Monday.\n7505\t(d/ decide-01\n7506\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7507\t\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n7508\t\t\t:ref-number Plural)\n7509\t\t:ARG1 (l/ leave-01\n7510\t\t\t:ARG0 p\n7511\t\t\t:temporal (d/ date-entity\n7512\t\t\t\t:weekday (m/ Monday))\n7513\t\t\t:aspect Performance\n7514\t\t\t:modpred d)\n7515\t\t:aspect Performance \n7516\t\t:modstr PrtAff) \n7517\t(s/ sentence\n7518\t\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1d)\n7519\t\t\t   (s1m :contained s1l))\n7520\t\t:modal ((AUTH :PrtAff s1p)\n7521\t\t\t(s1p :FullAff s1d)\n7522\t\t\t(s1d :Unsp s1l)))\n7523\t```\n7524\t<span id=\"4-3-1-2 (1d)\" label=\"4-3-1-2 (1d)\">4-3-1-2 (1d)</span> \n7525\t```\n7526\tHis parents forbid him from smoking.\n7527\t(f/ forbid-01\n7528\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7529\t\t\t:ARG0-of (k/ kinship)\n7530\t\t\t\t:ARG1 (p2/ person\n7531\t\t\t\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n7532\t\t\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7533\t\t\t\t:ARG2 (p3/ parent)\n7534\t\t\t:ref-number Plural)\n7535\t\t:ARG1 (s/ smoke-01\n7536\t\t\t:ARG0 p2\n7537\t\t\t:aspect Process\n7538\t\t\t:modpred f)\n7539\t\t:ARG2 p2\n7540\t\t:aspect State\n7541\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n7542\t(s/ sentence\n7543\t\t:temporal ((DCT :overlap s1f)\n7544\t\t\t   (s1f :overlap s1s))\n7545\t\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1p)\n7546\t\t\t(s1p :FullAff s1f)\n7547\t\t\t(s1f :Unsp s1s)))\n7548\t```\n7549\t\n7550\tNote that the modal itself is annotated with a\n7551\t`:modstr` value (if it is not under the\n7552\tscope of another modal). The actual modal value imparted by the modal\n7553\tevent is not annotated at Stage 0. It will be taken up in the lexical entries of modal complement-taking predicates and space-builders as the lexicon is being built, and will then automatically replace the unspecified link between the modal event and the modalized event in the document-level structure.\n7554\t\n7555\t[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n7556\t\n7557\t##### Part 4-3-1-3. quot relation\n7558\t\n7559\tEvents under the scope of a reporting predicate or a speech predicate\n7560\tare annotated with a `:quot` relation to the\n7561\treporting or speech predicate. Unlike events under the scope of modals,\n7562\tthese events are also annotated with a\n7563\t`:modstr` value.\n7564\t\n7565\t<span id=\"4-3-1-3 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-3 (1)\">4-3-1-3 (1)</span>\n7566\t\n7567\t<span id=\"4-3-1-3 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-3 (1)\">4-3-1-3 (1)</span> \n7568\t```\n7569\tMary said that she went to Santa Fe.\n7570\t(s/ say-01\n7571\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7572\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n7573\t\t:ARG1 (g/ go-01\n7574\t\t\t:ARG1 p\n7575\t\t\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n7576\t\t\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n7577\t\t\t\t:name (n2/ name\n7578\t\t\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n7579\t\t\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe))\n7580\t\t\t:aspect Performance\n7581\t\t\t:modstr FullAff\n7582\t\t\t:quot s\n7583\t\t:aspect Performance\n7584\t\t:modstr FullAff) \n7585\t(s/ sentence\n7586\t\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1s)\n7587\t\t\t   (s1s :before s1g))\n7588\t\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1s)\n7589\t\t\t(AUTH :FullAff s1p)\n7590\t\t\t(s1p :FullAff s1g)))\n7591\t```\n7592\t<span id=\"4-3-1-3 (1a)\" label=\"4-3-1-3 (1a)\">4-3-1-3 (1a)</span> \n7593\t```\n7594\tThe New York Times reported that Congress voted on the bill this afternoon.\n7595\t(r/ report-01\n7596\t\t:ARG0 (n/ newspaper\n7597\t\t\t:name (n2/ name\n7598\t\t\t\t:op1 \"The\"\n7599\t\t\t\t:op2 \"New\"\n7600\t\t\t\t:op3 \"York\"\n7601\t\t\t\t:op4 \"Times\")\n7602\t\t\t:wiki \"The_New_York_Times\")\n7603\t\t:ARG1 (v/ vote-01\n7604\t\t\t:ARG0 (g/ government-organization\n7605\t\t\t\t:name (n3/ name :op1 \"Congress\")\n7606\t\t\t\t:wiki \"United_States_Congress\")\n7607\t\t\t:ARG1 (b/ bill\n7608\t\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7609\t\t\t:temporal (a/ afternoon\n7610\t\t\t\t:mod (t/ this))\n7611\t\t\t:aspect Performance\n7612\t\t\t:modstr FullAff\n7613\t\t\t:quot r)\n7614\t\t:aspect Performance\n7615\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n7616\t(s/ sentence\n7617\t\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1r)\n7618\t\t\t   (s1a :contained s1v))\n7619\t\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1r)\n7620\t\t\t(AUTH :FullAff s1n)\n7621\t\t\t(s1n :FullAff s1v)))\n7622\t```\n7623\t<span id=\"4-3-1-3 (1b)\" label=\"4-3-1-3 (1b)\">4-3-1-3 (1b)</span> \n7624\t```\n7625\tMary might have said that she went to Santa Fe.\n7626\t(s/ say-01\n7627\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7628\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n7629\t\t:ARG1 (g/ go-01\n7630\t\t\t:ARG1 p\n7631\t\t\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n7632\t\t\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n7633\t\t\t\t:name (n2/ name\n7634\t\t\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n7635\t\t\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe))\n7636\t\t\t:aspect Performance\n7637\t\t\t:modstr FullAff\n7638\t\t\t:quot s\n7639\t\t:aspect Performance\n7640\t\t:modstr NeutAff) \n7641\t(s/ sentence\n7642\t\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1s)\n7643\t\t\t   (s1s :before s1g))\n7644\t\t:modal ((AUTH :NeutAff s1s)\n7645\t\t\t(AUTH :NeutAff s1p)\n7646\t\t\t(s1p :FullAff s1g))) \n7647\t```\n7648\t<span id=\"4-3-1-3 (1c)\" label=\"4-3-1-3 (1c)\">4-3-1-3 (1c)</span> \n7649\t```\n7650\tMary didn’t say that she went to Santa Fe.\n7651\t(s/ say-01\n7652\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7653\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n7654\t\t:ARG1 (g/ go-01\n7655\t\t\t:ARG1 p\n7656\t\t\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n7657\t\t\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n7658\t\t\t\t:name (n2/ name\n7659\t\t\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n7660\t\t\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe))\n7661\t\t\t:aspect Performance\n7662\t\t\t:modstr FullAff\n7663\t\t\t:quot s\n7664\t\t:aspect Performance\n7665\t\t:modstr FullNeg) \n7666\t(s/ sentence\n7667\t\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1s)\n7668\t\t\t   (s1s :before s1g))\n7669\t\t:modal ((AUTH :FullNeg s1s)\n7670\t\t\t(AUTH :FullNeg s1p)\n7671\t\t\t(s1p :FullAff s1g))) \n7672\t```\n7673\t<span id=\"4-3-1-3 (1d)\" label=\"4-3-1-3 (1d)\">4-3-1-3 (1d)</span> \n7674\t```\n7675\tMary said that John might have gone to Santa Fe.\n7676\t(s/ say-01\n7677\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7678\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n7679\t\t:ARG1 (g/ go-01\n7680\t\t\t:ARG1 (p2/ person\n7681\t\t\t\t:name (n2/ name :op1 \"John\"))\n7682\t\t\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n7683\t\t\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n7684\t\t\t\t:name (n3/ name\n7685\t\t\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n7686\t\t\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe))\n7687\t\t\t:aspect Performance\n7688\t\t\t:modstr NeutAff\n7689\t\t\t:quot s\n7690\t\t:aspect Performance\n7691\t\t:modstr FullAff) \n7692\t(s/ sentence\n7693\t\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1s)\n7694\t\t\t   (s1s :before s1g))\n7695\t\t:modal ((AUTH :FullNeg s1s)\n7696\t\t\t(AUTH :NeutAff s1p)\n7697\t\t\t(s1p :NeutAff s1g))) \n7698\t```\n7699\t<span id=\"4-3-1-3 (1e)\" label=\"4-3-1-3 (1e)\">4-3-1-3 (1e)</span> \n7700\t```\n7701\tMary said that John probably didn’t go to Santa Fe.\n7702\t(s/ say-01\n7703\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7704\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n7705\t\t:ARG1 (g/ go-01\n7706\t\t\t:ARG1 (p2/ person\n7707\t\t\t\t:name (n2/ name :op1 \"John\"))\n7708\t\t\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n7709\t\t\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n7710\t\t\t\t:name (n3/ name\n7711\t\t\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n7712\t\t\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe))\n7713\t\t\t:aspect Performance\n7714\t\t\t:modstr PrtNeg\n7715\t\t\t:quot s\n7716\t\t:aspect Performance\n7717\t\t:modstr FullAff) \n7718\t(s/ sentence\n7719\t\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1s)\n7720\t\t\t   (s1s :before s1g))\n7721\t\t:modal ((AUTH :FullNeg s1s)\n7722\t\t\t(AUTH :PrtNeg s1p)\n7723\t\t\t(s1p :PrtNeg s1g)))  \n7724\t```\n7725\tAs can be seen above, both the reporting predicate and the reported\n7726\tevents are annotated with a `:modstr` value.\n7727\tThe `:modstr` value of the reporting\n7728\tpredicate corresponds to the author’s certainty that the reporting event\n7729\thappened. The `:modstr` value associated\n7730\twith the reported events corresponds to the certainty with which the\n7731\tsayer/reporter reports the events. For example, in\n7732\t<a href=\"#4-3-1-3 (1d)\">(1d)</a>, the author is certain about the saying event,\n7733\tso it is annotated with `Aff`; but Mary is\n7734\tunsure about the reality of the going event, and therefore it is\n7735\tannotated with `Neut` modal strength.\n7736\t\n7737\t[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n7738\t\n7739\t##### Part 4-3-1-4. purpose relation\n7740\t\n7741\tEvents in purpose clauses are annotated with both a\n7742\t`:modstr` value in the document-level annotation, and with the `:purpose` participant role relation to the main clause event in the sentence-level annotation.\n7743\t\n7744\t<span id=\"4-3-1-4 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-4 (1)\">4-3-1-4 (1)</span>\n7745\t```\n7746\tThey dropped water in order to fight the fire.\n7747\t(d/ drop-01\n7748\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7749\t\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n7750\t\t\t:ref-number Plural)\n7751\t\t:ARG1 (w/ water)\n7752\t\t:purpose (f/ fight-01\n7753\t\t\t:ARG0 p\n7754\t\t\t:ARG1 (f/ fire)\n7755\t\t\t:aspect Activity\n7756\t\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n7757\t\t:aspect Performance\n7758\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n7759\t(s/ sentence\n7760\t\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1d)\n7761\t\t\t   (s1d :after :s1f))\n7762\t\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1d)\n7763\t\t\t(AUTH :FullAff s1p)\n7764\t\t\t(s1p :PrtAff purpose)\n7765\t\t\t(purpose :FullAff s1f)))\n7766\t```\n7767\t<span id=\"4-3-1-4 (2)\" label=\"4-3-1-4 (2)\">4-3-1-4 (2)</span>\n7768\t```\n7769\tHe walked quickly in order to not arrive late.\n7770\t(w/ walk-01\n7771\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7772\t\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n7773\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7774\t\t:manner (q/ quickly)\n7775\t\t:purpose (a/ arrive-01\n7776\t\t\t:ARG1 p\n7777\t\t\t:temporal (l/ late)\n7778\t\t\t:aspect Performance\n7779\t\t\t:modstr FullNeg)\n7780\t\t:aspect Activity\n7781\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n7782\t(s/ sentence\n7783\t\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1w)\n7784\t\t\t   (s1w :after s1a))\n7785\t\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1w)\n7786\t\t\t(AUTH :FullAff s1p)\n7787\t\t\t(s1p :PrtAff purpose)\n7788\t\t\t(purpose :FullNeg s1a)))\n7789\t```\n7790\t\n7791\tThe `:modstr` value represents any modals or\n7792\tnegation that are present within the purpose clause. That is, this value\n7793\tdoesn’t capture the fact that the purpose clause itself imparts a\n7794\tnon-full epistemic stance on its events; that is captured by the\n7795\t`:purpose` relation.\n7796\t\n7797\t[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n7798\t\n7799\t##### Part 4-3-1-5. condition relation\n7800\t\n7801\tThe `:condition` relation in the sentence-level annotation indicates the relationship between events in conditional constructions. These events\n7802\talso receive a `:modstr` annotation. As can\n7803\tbe seen in <a href=\"#4-3-1-5 (1)\">(1)</a>, the event in the protasis is annotated with a `:condition` relation to the event in the\n7804\tapodosis.\n7805\t\n7806\t<span id=\"4-3-1-5 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-5 (1)\">4-3-1-5 (1)</span>\n7807\t\n7808\t<span id=\"4-3-1-5 (1a)\" label=\"4-3-1-5 (1a)\">4-3-1-5 (1a)</span> \n7809\t```\n7810\tIf she’s hungry, I’ll feed her dinner.\n7811\t(f/ feed-01\n7812\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7813\t\t\t:ref-person 1st\n7814\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7815\t\t:ARG1 (d/ dinner)\n7816\t\t:ARG2 (p2/ person\n7817\t\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n7818\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7819\t\t:condition (h/ hunger-01\n7820\t\t\t:ARG0 p2\n7821\t\t\t:aspect State\n7822\t\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n7823\t\t:aspect Performance\n7824\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n7825\t(s/ sentence\n7826\t\t:temporal ((DCT :overlap s1h)\n7827\t\t\t   (DCT :after s1f))\n7828\t\t:modal ((AUTH :NeutAff have-condition)\n7829\t\t\t(have-condition :FullAff s1h)\n7830\t\t\t(have-condition :FullAff s1f)))\n7831\t```\n7832\t<span id=\"4-3-1-5 (1b)\" label=\"4-3-1-5 (1b)\">4-3-1-5 (1b)</span> \n7833\t```\n7834\tIf she’s hungry, maybe I’ll cook pasta.\n7835\t(c/ cook-01\n7836\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7837\t\t\t:ref-person 1st\n7838\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7839\t\t:ARG1 (p2/ pasta)\n7840\t\t:condition (h/ hunger-01\n7841\t\t\t:ARG0 (p3/ person\n7842\t\t\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n7843\t\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7844\t\t\t:aspect State\n7845\t\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n7846\t\t:aspect Performance\n7847\t\t:modstr NeutAff)\n7848\t(s/ sentence\n7849\t\t:temporal ((DCT :overlap s1h)\n7850\t\t\t   (DCT :after s1c))\n7851\t\t:modal ((AUTH :NeutAff have-condition)\n7852\t\t\t(have-condition :FullAff s1h)\n7853\t\t\t(have-condition :NeutAff s1c)))\n7854\t```\n7855\t<span id=\"4-3-1-5 (1c)\" label=\"4-3-1-5 (1c)\">4-3-1-5 (1c)</span> \n7856\t```\n7857\tIf she isn’t hungry, we’ll just watch a movie.\n7858\t(w/ watch-01\n7859\t\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n7860\t\t\t:ref-person 1st\n7861\t\t\t:ref-number Plural)\n7862\t\t:ARG1 (m/ movie)\n7863\t\t:mod (j/ just)\n7864\t\t:condition (h/ hunger-01\n7865\t\t\t:ARG0 (p2/ person\n7866\t\t\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n7867\t\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n7868\t\t\t:aspect State\n7869\t\t\t:modstr FullNeg)\n7870\t\t:aspect Performance\n7871\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n7872\t(s/ sentence\n7873\t\t:temporal ((DCT :overlap s1h)\n7874\t\t\t   (DCT :after s1w))\n7875\t\t:modal ((AUTH :NeutAff have-condition)\n7876\t\t\t(have-condition :FullNeg s1h)\n7877\t\t\t(have-condition :FullAff s1c))) \n7878\t```\n7879\tAs with purpose clauses, the `:modstr` value\n7880\tdoesn’t capture the uncertainty imparted by the conditional construction\n7881\titself; it corresponds to any negation or modals which are expressed\n7882\tinside of the conditional construction. The modal value of the\n7883\tconditional construction is captured by the\n7884\t`:condition` value.\n7885\t\n7886\t[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n7887\t\n7888\t##### Part 4-3-1-6. Modal dependency structure\n7889\t\n7890\tAt Stage 0, there are some pieces of the modal dependency structure that\n7891\tare unspecified. The modal strength that a modal verb imparts on its\n7892\tcomplement is one of these pieces. As the modal annotation progresses,\n7893\tthis information is added to the frame files for modal verbs. For\n7894\texample, the complements of English *want* have a\n7895\t`:modstr NeutAff` value; this will be indicated\n7896\tin the frame file as shown below.\n7897\t\n7898\t```\n7899\tPredicate: want.01 \n7900\t\tRoles:  \n7901\t\tArg0: wanter  \n7902\t\tArg1: thing wanted  \n7903\t\tArg2: beneficiary  \n7904\t\tArg3: in-exchange-for  \n7905\t\tArg4: from \n7906\t\n7907\t:modstr of complement: NeutAff  \n7908\t```\n7909\t\n7910\tFor the complements of modals, the `:modstr`\n7911\tvalues work largely the same way that they do for other events; however,\n7912\tthey reflect the beliefs of the\n7913\t`:experiencer` participant of the modal\n7914\tevent, who is often not the author. For example, in\n7915\t<a href=\"#4-3-1-6 (1)\">(1)</a>, Mary believes that the visit event may take\n7916\tplace in the future (`NeutAff` strength), but\n7917\tthe author disagrees.\n7918\t\n7919\t<span id=\"4-3-1-6 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-6 (1)\">\\[4-3-1-6 (1)\\]</span> Mary wants\n7920\tto visit France next month, but I don’t think that’s possible.\n7921\t\n7922\tThe value associated with the modal event in its frame file corresponds\n7923\tto Mary’s beliefs, as the `:experiencer` of\n7924\tthe wanting event.\n7925\t\n7926\tSome predicates impart full, positive\n7927\t(`FullAff`) strength on their complements,\n7928\toften called factive predicates (e.g., *manage to*). Strong epistemic\n7929\tmodals (e.g., *expect that, deduce*) and strong deontic modals,\n7930\tincluding intention modals (e.g., *plan to, decide to*) and obligation\n7931\tmodals (e.g., *need, demand*), impart `PrtAff`\n7932\tstrength on their complements. Weak deontic modals, including desire\n7933\t(e.g., *want*) and permission (e.g., *allow*), impart\n7934\t`NeutAff` strength on their complements.\n7935\tCertain modals may also lexicalize negation, such as *doubt*, *forbid*,\n7936\tor *wish*. These are annotated with the\n7937\t`NeutNeg`,\n7938\t`PrtNeg`, and\n7939\t`FullNeg` values, respectively.\n7940\t\n7941\t[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n7942\t\n7943\t#### Part 4-3-2. English modals\n7944\t\n7945\tThis list gives the modal strength value associated with common English\n7946\tmodal constructions (this is certaintly not an exhaustive list). For\n7947\tmodal predicates that are identified as their own event (e.g.,\n7948\tdeontic predicates), the modal strength value characterizes the dependency link between the modal predicate node and its child event. For example, *want* is in the `NeutAff` list, which indicates that there\n7949\tis a `NeutAff` link between the `want` node and its\n7950\tcomplement event node in the full dependency structure.   \n7951\t\n7952\t`FullAff` (full affirmative)\n7953\t\n7954\t  - Simple assertions: declarative sentences\n7955\t\n7956\t  - Certainty: *certainly, be sure, definitely, necessarily*\n7957\t\n7958\t  - Predictive future: *will,* non-intentional *be going to*"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b1f75d6-aaf3-4c40-8364-7ab0d9bdc500","uuid":"49e72211-083c-47c5-82ba-c884e67e50ab","timestamp":"2026-11-10T07:29:33.432Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/umr-harness/s1-full/zh-0032/guidelines/umr_guidelines.md","content":"### Part 4-3. Modal Dependency\n\nUMR represents modal strength and polarity as a dependency structure.\nThe nodes are either events or conceivers (i.e., a source, an entity\nwhose perspective on an event is modelled in the text). The edges in the dependency structure correspond to modal strength and polarity values (i.e., how certain a specific conceiver is about the occurrence of the event in the real world). Annotators do not have to construct the dependency structure directly, but it can be built up “behind the\nscenes” by annotating some modal/polarity information and leveraging\nthe participant role annotation.\n\nThe modal annotation captures the modal strength of events, but not the modal type of the event (i.e., epistemic/evidential, deontic,\npermissive, etc.). These modal strengths are annotated based on a\nlattice of annotation values that differ in terms of granularity. An\nexample of the UMR annotation and the underlying modal dependency\nstructure is shown below in <a href=\"#4-3 (1)\">(1)</a>.\n\n<span id=\"4-3 (1)\" label=\"4-3 (1)\">4-3 (1)</span>\n\n![Modal dependency structure](https://github.com/umr4nlp/umr-guidelines/blob/master/Guidelines_figures/Modal%20Dep_2022.jpg)\n\n```\nMartin said that the package has probably already arrived.\n(s/ say-01  \n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Martin\")\n\t:ARG1 (a/ arrive-01  \n\t\t:ARG1 (p/ package\n\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)  \n\t\tquot: s\n\t\tmodstr: PrtAff)\n\tmodstr: Aff)\n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1s)\n\t\t   (s1s :before s1a))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1p)\n\t\t(AUTH :FullAff s1s)\n\t\t(s1p :PrtAff s1a)))\n```\n\nThe *say*-event and the *arrive*-event each get a modal strength value (`:modstr`), which can then be represented\nin the dependency as an edge between the event and the relevant conceiver/source. The\n`:quot` value indicates than an event is\nbeing reported, and the participant role annotation can be used to\nautomatically select the conceiver for the reported event(s), here,\n*Martin*.\n\nThis simplified, mostly sentence-level, annotation process is step 0 of the road map for modal annotation. Step 1 builds on this step to end up at a fully specified modal dependency structure. This means that Stage 1 annotation involves first doing the Stage 0 modality annotations. (This contrasts with participant roles, where annotators can largely ignore Stage 0 if the annotation language has existing frame files.)\n\n[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n\n#### Part 4-3-1. Stage 0\n\nThere are two types of modal annotations at Stage 0: a\n`:modstr` annotation that consists of a\nsingle epistemic strength/polarity value, and a dependency annotation\nthat indicates a relation between two events. There are two dependency\nrelations: `:modpred` for the link between a\nmodal event and the event(s) that it modalizes, and\n`:quot` for the link between a reporting\nevent and the event(s) that it reports. Additionally, events in purpose clauses and events in conditional constructions must be taken up in the modal dependency tree. The right structure of the modal dependency graph for these events will be extrapolated from the sentence-level annotation: events in purpose clauses are daughters of events in the main clause they depend on, and are connected to them with the `:purpose` participant role. This can be leveraged to embed the purpose-event underneath the correct parent in the modal dependency graph as well. For conditionals, similarly, the protasis event will be embedded under the apodosis event with a `:condition` relation, or the protasis and apodosis events will both be ```:ARG1``` and ```:ARG2``` of a `have-condition-91` node. Again, this information will be taken up in the eventual modal dependency graph without annotators needing to indicate it a second time in the document-level modal annotation workflow.\nThe `:modstr` annotation applies to all events, except for those under the scope of a modal identified as its own event (i.e., events with `:mod` relations). This\nis summarized below.\n\n<div id=\"tab:modal_relations\">\n\n| **Event type** | **UMR Treatment** |\n| :------------------------------- |:------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Events under the scope of modals | `:modpred` relation to CPT |\n| Events under the scope of reporting events| `:modstr` annotation, `:quot` relation to reporting event |\n| All other events | `:modstr` annotation |\n\nTable 14: Modal relations at Stage 0\n</div>\n\n[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n\n##### Part 4-3-1-1. modstr values\n\nThe modal strength values correspond to epistemic strength, i.e. the\nauthor or conceiver’s certainty about the occurrence of the event in the real world, or certainty about another conceiver’s mental content. Based on Boye (2012), a typological study of modal systems across languages, and following FactBank (Pustejovsky et al. 2005), the UMR annotation is based around three levels of modal strength: `Full`, `Partial`, and `Neutral`, illustrated in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1 (1)\">(1)</a>.\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-1 (1)\">4-3-1-1 (1)</span>\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1 (1a)\" label=\"4-3-1-1 (1a)\">4-3-1-1 (1a)</span> Full:  \nThe cat already **ate** breakfast.\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1 (1b)\" label=\"4-3-1-1 (1b)\">4-3-1-1 (1b)</span>\nPartial:  \nThe cat <u>probably</u> already **ate** breakfast.  \n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1 (1c)\" label=\"4-3-1-1 (1c)\">4-3-1-1 (1c)</span>\nNeutral:  \nThe cat <u>might</u> have already **eaten** breakfast.\n\nThe `Full` modal strength value, as in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1 (1a)\">(1a)</a>, corresponds to complete certainty; that is, the conceiver is 100% certain that the event occurs in the real world. The `Neutral` modal strength value, shown in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1 (1c)\">(1c)</a>, indicates the possibility of the event; essentially, this corresponds to 50/50 certainty that the event occurs in the real world. The `Partial` modal strength value, as in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1 (1b)\">(1b)</a>, falls between the `Full` and `Neutral` values; the conceiver believes that more likely than not, the event occurs in the real world.\n\nBut, `Full`, `Partial`, and `Neutral` aren’t the only possible modal strength annotation values. Languages differ in the modal strength distinctions that are conventionalized in their grammar. In order to accommodate these differences, we use a typological lattice of annotation values, constructed based on the structure of the annotation category across languages (Van Gysel et al. 2019).\n\nOne level of granularity in the lattice is designated as the “base\nlevel”: annotators are encouraged to use categories from this level as\nthe default. These values are selected as the base level because these\ndistinctions occur the most frequently across languages. The higher and lower levels, respectively, contain equally typologically-motivated coarser-grained and finer-grained categories, which can be used when a language conventionalizes these distinctions in its grammar. Such lattices capture the idea that many semantic categories are structured as hierarchical scales, where the middle values can group together with either end, but the extremes of the scale are highly unlikely to be categorized together in any language. For example, no language has a grammatical form that is used for both `Full` and `Neutral` epistemic strength, but not `Partial`. The typological lattice for epistemic strength is shown below.\n\n![Modal strength lattice](https://github.com/umr4nlp/umr-guidelines/blob/master/Guidelines_figures/Affirmative%20Modal%20Strength%20Lattice_2022.png)\n\n![Modal strength lattice](https://github.com/umr4nlp/umr-guidelines/blob/master/Guidelines_figures/Negative%20Modal%20Strength%20Lattice_2022.png)\n\nThis lattice is based around the base level of `Full` vs. `Partial` vs.\n`Neutral`, but also allows for the annotation of more coarse-grained\nvalues that lump together the distinctions in the base level, and more\nfine-grained annotation values. For contexts where it is unclear if the modal strength is `Full` or `Partial`, the `Non-neutral` value can be used; if it is unclear whether the modal strength is `Partial` or `Neutral`, then the `Non-full` value can be used. The most fine-grained modal strength values are generally used with languages that have grammatical forms that encode the relevant distinction.\n\nAlso following FactBank (Pustejovsky et al. 2006), the `:modstr`\nannotation combines the epistemic strength values with a binary polarity distinction (`Affirmative`, `Negative`). This results in six modal strength/polarity values for the default level, shown below. Both the affirmative values and the negative values have their own set of related coarse-grained and fine-grained values.\n\n<div>\n\n| **Label**                              | **Value**                              |\n| :------------------------------------- | :------------------------------------- |\n| ```FullAff```     | full strength, affirmative polarity    |\n| ```PrtAff```     | partial strength, affirmative polarity |\n| ```NeutAFf```    | neutral strength, affirmative polarity |\n| ```NeutNeg``` | neutral strength, negative polarity    |\n| ```PrtNeg```  | partial strength, negative polarity    |\n| ```FullNeg```    | full strength, negative polarity       |\n\n</div>\n\nThese values and their interpretation are shown below; the corresponding FactBank values are in parentheses.\n\n```FullAff```: full affirmative support; complete certainty that the event occurs (CT+)  \n```PrtAff```: partial affirmative support; there is strong, but not definitive certainty that the event occurs (PR+)  \n```NeutAff```: affirmative neutral support; there is neutral certainty that the event occurs/doesn’t occur; event is expressed positively (PS+)  \n```NeutNeg```: negative neutral support; there is neutral certainty that the event occurs/doesn’t occur; negation of event is expressed (PS-)  \n```PrtNeg```: partial negative support; there is strong but not definitive certainty that the event does not occur (PR-)  \n```FullNeg```: full negative support; complete certainty that the event does not occur (CT-)\n\nDegree of certainty corresponds most straightforwardly to the degree of confidence of a conceiver (often, the author) in the occurrence of an episodic event, i.e. the epistemic continuum from certainty to\npossibility. We use these same values for the evidential continuum from direct evidence to second-hand (reported or inferred) evidence; see [Part 4-3-1-1-2](#part-4-3-1-1-2-evidential-justification) below). And these values are\ninterpreted into the domain of future-oriented or deontic modality, as\nexplained in [Part 4-3-1-1-3](#part-4-3-1-1-3-future-events-and-deontic-modality). The interpretation of\nthe value - as epistemic, evidential or deontic - is not reflected in\nthe modal strength annotation.\n\n[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n\n##### Part 4-3-1-1-1. Non-future events\n\nFor non-future (non-deontic) events, the `modstr` values correspond to the author’s level of certainty towards the occurrence of the event in the real world. Events presented as fact are annotated with\n`FullAff`, while events for which the author categorically denies their occurrence are annotated `FullNeg`. When the author doesn’t present the\nevent as fact, but has a higher level of certainty towards the event\neither being true or not true, this is annotated as\n`PrtAff` or, when the polarity is negative, `PrtNeg`. When the author doesn’t lean either direction towards the event being true in the real world or not, the event is annotated as `NeutAff` or\n`NeutNeg`, depending on the polarity of the\nlinguistic expression. These strength values are exemplified in\n<a href=\"#4-3-1-1-1 (1)\">(1)</a>.\n\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1)\">4-3-1-1-1 (1)</span>\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1a)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1a)\">4-3-1-1-1 (1a)</span>   \n```\nThe dog barked last night.\n(b/ bark-01\n\t:ARG0 (d/ dog\n\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t:temporal (n/ night\n\t\t:mod (l/ last))\n\t:aspect Endeavor\n\t:modstr FullAff)  \n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1b)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1b)\">4-3-1-1-1 (1b)</span>  \n```\nThe dog probably barked last night.\n(b/ bark-01\n\t:ARG0 (d/ dog\n\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t:temporal (n/ night\n\t\t:mod (l/ last))\n\t:aspect Endeavor\n\t:modstr PrtAff)  \n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1c)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1c)\">4-3-1-1-1 (1c)</span>\n```\nThe dog may have barked last night.\n(b/ bark-01\n\t:ARG0 (d/ dog\n\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t:temporal (n/ night\n\t\t:mod (l/ last))\n\t:aspect Endeavor\n\t:modstr NeutAff)  \n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1d)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1d)\">4-3-1-1-1 (1d)</span> \n```\nThe dog may not have barked last night.\n(b/ bark-01\n\t:ARG0 (d/ dog\n\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t:temporal (n/ night\n\t\t:mod (l/ last))\n\t:aspect Endeavor\n\t:modstr NeutNeg)  \n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1e)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1e)\">4-3-1-1-1 (1e)</span>\n```\nThe dog probably didn't bark last night.\n(b/ bark-01\n\t:ARG0 (d/ dog\n\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t:temporal (n/ night\n\t\t:mod (l/ last))\n\t:aspect Endeavor\n\t:modstr PrtNeg)  \n``` \n<span id=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1f)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-1 (1f)\">4-3-1-1-1 (1f)</span>\n```   \nThe dog didn't bark last night.\n(b/ bark-01\n\t:ARG0 (d/ dog\n\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t:temporal (n/ night\n\t\t:mod (l/ last))\n\t:aspect Endeavor\n\t:modstr FullNeg)  \n```  \n[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n\n##### Part 4-3-1-1-2. Evidential justification\n\nFollowing Boye (2012) and Saurí and Pustejovsky (2009), we conflate evidential justification with epistemic support. Boye (2012) finds that there is cross-linguistic evidence for lumping epistemic support and evidential justification together into the same relations. Languages may encode direct evidential justification (sensory perception) with the same forms as full epistemic support; indirect justification (hearsay, inferential) may be encoded by the same forms as partial epistemic support.\n\nExample <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-2 (1)\">(1)</a> shows how direct and indirect justification correspond to epistemic support.\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1-2 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-2 (1)\">4-3-1-1-2 (1)</span>\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1-2 (1a)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-2 (1a)\">4-3-1-1-2 (1a)</span> \n```\n(I saw) Mary feed the cat.\n(s/ see-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:ref-person 1st\n\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t:ARG1 (f/ feed-01\n\t\t:ARG0 (p2/ person\n\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n\t\t:ARG2 (c/ cat\n\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t\t:aspect Performance\n\t\t**:modstr FullAff**)\n\t:aspect State\n\t:modstr FullAff)\n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1-2 (1b)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-2 (1b)\">4-3-1-1-2 (1b)</span> \n```\nMary must have fed the cat.\n(f/ feed-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n\t:ARG2 (c/ cat\n\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t:aspect Performance\n\t:modstr PrtAff)\n```\n\nIn <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-2 (1a)\">(1a)</a>, the author has direct knowledge of the feeding event, by way of witnessing it. Therefore, *feed* is annotated with `Aff` modal strength. In <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-2 (1b)\">(1b)</a>, however, *must* signals that the author is inferring that the feeding event occurred without direct, perceptual knowledge. Therefore, *fed* in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-2 (1b)\">(1b)</a> is annotated with `Prt` modal strength.\n\n[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n\n##### Part 4-3-1-1-3. Future events and deontic modality\n\nFor events presented as (potentially) happening in the future,\n`:modstr` refers to the predictability of\nthe occurrence of the event in the future, as presented by the author.\nPredictive future has full strength (`FullAff`\nor `FullNeg`); intentions and commands\ncorrespond to partial strength (`PrtAff` or\n`PrtNeg`); and desire and permission\ncorrespond to neutral (`NeutAff` or\n`NeutNeg`) strength. Keep in mind that events under the scope of modals identified as their own event don't receive any `:modstr` value at all. This section refers to deontic meanings indicated by grammaticalized modals that don't fit the criteria to be identified as events.\n\nThis is illustrated in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-3 (1)\">(1)</a>.\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1)\">4-3-1-1-3 (1)</span>\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1a)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1a)\">4-3-1-1-3 (1a)</span>  \n```\nI will go to Santa Fe.\n(g/ go-01\n\t:ARG1 (p/ person\n\t\t:ref-person 1st\n\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n\t\t:name (n/ name\n\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe\"))\n\t:aspect Performance\n\t:modstr FullAff)\n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1b)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1b)\">4-3-1-1-3 (1b)</span>   \n```\nYou must go to Santa Fe.\n(g/ go-01\n\t:ARG1 (p/ person\n\t\t:ref-person 2nd\n\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n\t\t:name (n/ name\n\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe\"))\n\t:aspect Performance\n\t:modstr PrtAff\n\t:mode Imperative)\n```\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1c)\" label=\"4-3-1-1-3 (1c)\">4-3-1-1-3 (1c)</span>   \n```\nYou can go to Santa Fe.\n(g/ go-01\n\t:ARG1 (p/ person\n\t\t:ref-person 2nd\n\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n\t\t:name (n/ name\n\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe\"))\n\t:aspect Performance\n\t:modstr NeutAff)\n```\nThe predictive future, as in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-3 (1a)\">(1a)</a>, is annotated with\nfull modal strength because it presents the future event as a certainty\n(i.e., it is as certain as is possible for future events). Commands, as\nin <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-3 (1b)\">(1b)</a>, are annotated with partial modal strength\nbecause they present the future event as less likely than the predictive\nfuture, but more likely to happen than the neutral strength deontics.\nFinally, permission, as in <a href=\"#4-3-1-1-3 (1c)\">(1c)</a>, is annotated as\nneutral strength, since even if someone has permission to do something, there is no guarantee they will.\n\n[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n\n##### Part 4-3-1-2. modpred relation\n\nEvents under the scope of a modal identified as its own event are only\nannotated with a `:modpred` relation to the\nrelevant modal. This is shown below in <a href=\"#4-3-1-2 (1)\">(1)</a>.\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-2 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-2 (1)\">4-3-1-2 (1)</span>\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-2 (1a)\" label=\"4-3-1-2 (1a)\">4-3-1-2 (1a)</span> \n```\nMary wants to visit France.\n(w/ want-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n\t:ARG1 (v/ visit-01\n\t\t:ARG0 p\n\t\t:ARG1 (c/ country\n\t\t\t:wiki \"France\"\n\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"France\"))\n\t\t:aspect Performance\n\t\t:modpred w\n\t:aspect State\n\t:modstr FullAff\n\n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((DCT :overlap s1w)\n\t\t   (s1w :after s1v))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1p)\n\t\t(s1p :FullAff s1w)\n\t\t(s1w :Unsp s1v))\n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-2 (1b)\" label=\"4-3-1-2 (1b)\">4-3-1-2 (1b)</span> \n```\nRob thinks the dog escaped through the fence.\n(t/ think-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Rob\"))\n\t:ARG1 (e/ escape-01\n\t\t:ARG0 (d/ dog\n\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t\t:path (f/ fence)\n\t\t:aspect Performance\n\t\t:modpred t)\n\t:aspect State\n\t:modstr FullAff)\n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((DCT :overlap s1t)\n\t\t   (s1t :before s1e))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1p)\n\t\t(s1p :FullAff s1t)\n\t\t(s1t :Unsp s1e)))\n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-2 (1c)\" label=\"4-3-1-2 (1c)\">4-3-1-2 (1c)</span> \n```\nThey probably decided to leave on Monday.\n(d/ decide-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n\t\t:ref-number Plural)\n\t:ARG1 (l/ leave-01\n\t\t:ARG0 p\n\t\t:temporal (d/ date-entity\n\t\t\t:weekday (m/ Monday))\n\t\t:aspect Performance\n\t\t:modpred d)\n\t:aspect Performance \n\t:modstr PrtAff) \n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1d)\n\t\t   (s1m :contained s1l))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :PrtAff s1p)\n\t\t(s1p :FullAff s1d)\n\t\t(s1d :Unsp s1l)))\n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-2 (1d)\" label=\"4-3-1-2 (1d)\">4-3-1-2 (1d)</span> \n```\nHis parents forbid him from smoking.\n(f/ forbid-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:ARG0-of (k/ kinship)\n\t\t\t:ARG1 (p2/ person\n\t\t\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n\t\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t\t\t:ARG2 (p3/ parent)\n\t\t:ref-number Plural)\n\t:ARG1 (s/ smoke-01\n\t\t:ARG0 p2\n\t\t:aspect Process\n\t\t:modpred f)\n\t:ARG2 p2\n\t:aspect State\n\t:modstr FullAff)\n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((DCT :overlap s1f)\n\t\t   (s1f :overlap s1s))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1p)\n\t\t(s1p :FullAff s1f)\n\t\t(s1f :Unsp s1s)))\n```\n\nNote that the modal itself is annotated with a\n`:modstr` value (if it is not under the\nscope of another modal). The actual modal value imparted by the modal\nevent is not annotated at Stage 0. It will be taken up in the lexical entries of modal complement-taking predicates and space-builders as the lexicon is being built, and will then automatically replace the unspecified link between the modal event and the modalized event in the document-level structure.\n\n[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n\n##### Part 4-3-1-3. quot relation\n\nEvents under the scope of a reporting predicate or a speech predicate\nare annotated with a `:quot` relation to the\nreporting or speech predicate. Unlike events under the scope of modals,\nthese events are also annotated with a\n`:modstr` value.\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-3 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-3 (1)\">4-3-1-3 (1)</span>\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-3 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-3 (1)\">4-3-1-3 (1)</span> \n```\nMary said that she went to Santa Fe.\n(s/ say-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n\t:ARG1 (g/ go-01\n\t\t:ARG1 p\n\t\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n\t\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n\t\t\t:name (n2/ name\n\t\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n\t\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe))\n\t\t:aspect Performance\n\t\t:modstr FullAff\n\t\t:quot s\n\t:aspect Performance\n\t:modstr FullAff) \n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1s)\n\t\t   (s1s :before s1g))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1s)\n\t\t(AUTH :FullAff s1p)\n\t\t(s1p :FullAff s1g)))\n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-3 (1a)\" label=\"4-3-1-3 (1a)\">4-3-1-3 (1a)</span> \n```\nThe New York Times reported that Congress voted on the bill this afternoon.\n(r/ report-01\n\t:ARG0 (n/ newspaper\n\t\t:name (n2/ name\n\t\t\t:op1 \"The\"\n\t\t\t:op2 \"New\"\n\t\t\t:op3 \"York\"\n\t\t\t:op4 \"Times\")\n\t\t:wiki \"The_New_York_Times\")\n\t:ARG1 (v/ vote-01\n\t\t:ARG0 (g/ government-organization\n\t\t\t:name (n3/ name :op1 \"Congress\")\n\t\t\t:wiki \"United_States_Congress\")\n\t\t:ARG1 (b/ bill\n\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t\t:temporal (a/ afternoon\n\t\t\t:mod (t/ this))\n\t\t:aspect Performance\n\t\t:modstr FullAff\n\t\t:quot r)\n\t:aspect Performance\n\t:modstr FullAff)\n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1r)\n\t\t   (s1a :contained s1v))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1r)\n\t\t(AUTH :FullAff s1n)\n\t\t(s1n :FullAff s1v)))\n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-3 (1b)\" label=\"4-3-1-3 (1b)\">4-3-1-3 (1b)</span> \n```\nMary might have said that she went to Santa Fe.\n(s/ say-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n\t:ARG1 (g/ go-01\n\t\t:ARG1 p\n\t\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n\t\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n\t\t\t:name (n2/ name\n\t\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n\t\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe))\n\t\t:aspect Performance\n\t\t:modstr FullAff\n\t\t:quot s\n\t:aspect Performance\n\t:modstr NeutAff) \n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1s)\n\t\t   (s1s :before s1g))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :NeutAff s1s)\n\t\t(AUTH :NeutAff s1p)\n\t\t(s1p :FullAff s1g))) \n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-3 (1c)\" label=\"4-3-1-3 (1c)\">4-3-1-3 (1c)</span> \n```\nMary didn’t say that she went to Santa Fe.\n(s/ say-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n\t:ARG1 (g/ go-01\n\t\t:ARG1 p\n\t\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n\t\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n\t\t\t:name (n2/ name\n\t\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n\t\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe))\n\t\t:aspect Performance\n\t\t:modstr FullAff\n\t\t:quot s\n\t:aspect Performance\n\t:modstr FullNeg) \n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1s)\n\t\t   (s1s :before s1g))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :FullNeg s1s)\n\t\t(AUTH :FullNeg s1p)\n\t\t(s1p :FullAff s1g))) \n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-3 (1d)\" label=\"4-3-1-3 (1d)\">4-3-1-3 (1d)</span> \n```\nMary said that John might have gone to Santa Fe.\n(s/ say-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n\t:ARG1 (g/ go-01\n\t\t:ARG1 (p2/ person\n\t\t\t:name (n2/ name :op1 \"John\"))\n\t\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n\t\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n\t\t\t:name (n3/ name\n\t\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n\t\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe))\n\t\t:aspect Performance\n\t\t:modstr NeutAff\n\t\t:quot s\n\t:aspect Performance\n\t:modstr FullAff) \n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1s)\n\t\t   (s1s :before s1g))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :FullNeg s1s)\n\t\t(AUTH :NeutAff s1p)\n\t\t(s1p :NeutAff s1g))) \n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-3 (1e)\" label=\"4-3-1-3 (1e)\">4-3-1-3 (1e)</span> \n```\nMary said that John probably didn’t go to Santa Fe.\n(s/ say-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Mary\"))\n\t:ARG1 (g/ go-01\n\t\t:ARG1 (p2/ person\n\t\t\t:name (n2/ name :op1 \"John\"))\n\t\t:ARG4 (c/ city\n\t\t\t:wiki \"Santa_Fe,_New_Mexico\"\n\t\t\t:name (n3/ name\n\t\t\t\t:op1 \"Santa\"\n\t\t\t\t:op2 \"Fe))\n\t\t:aspect Performance\n\t\t:modstr PrtNeg\n\t\t:quot s\n\t:aspect Performance\n\t:modstr FullAff) \n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1s)\n\t\t   (s1s :before s1g))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :FullNeg s1s)\n\t\t(AUTH :PrtNeg s1p)\n\t\t(s1p :PrtNeg s1g)))  \n```\nAs can be seen above, both the reporting predicate and the reported\nevents are annotated with a `:modstr` value.\nThe `:modstr` value of the reporting\npredicate corresponds to the author’s certainty that the reporting event\nhappened. The `:modstr` value associated\nwith the reported events corresponds to the certainty with which the\nsayer/reporter reports the events. For example, in\n<a href=\"#4-3-1-3 (1d)\">(1d)</a>, the author is certain about the saying event,\nso it is annotated with `Aff`; but Mary is\nunsure about the reality of the going event, and therefore it is\nannotated with `Neut` modal strength.\n\n[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n\n##### Part 4-3-1-4. purpose relation\n\nEvents in purpose clauses are annotated with both a\n`:modstr` value in the document-level annotation, and with the `:purpose` participant role relation to the main clause event in the sentence-level annotation.\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-4 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-4 (1)\">4-3-1-4 (1)</span>\n```\nThey dropped water in order to fight the fire.\n(d/ drop-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n\t\t:ref-number Plural)\n\t:ARG1 (w/ water)\n\t:purpose (f/ fight-01\n\t\t:ARG0 p\n\t\t:ARG1 (f/ fire)\n\t\t:aspect Activity\n\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n\t:aspect Performance\n\t:modstr FullAff)\n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1d)\n\t\t   (s1d :after :s1f))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1d)\n\t\t(AUTH :FullAff s1p)\n\t\t(s1p :PrtAff purpose)\n\t\t(purpose :FullAff s1f)))\n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-4 (2)\" label=\"4-3-1-4 (2)\">4-3-1-4 (2)</span>\n```\nHe walked quickly in order to not arrive late.\n(w/ walk-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t:manner (q/ quickly)\n\t:purpose (a/ arrive-01\n\t\t:ARG1 p\n\t\t:temporal (l/ late)\n\t\t:aspect Performance\n\t\t:modstr FullNeg)\n\t:aspect Activity\n\t:modstr FullAff)\n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((PAST_REF :contained s1w)\n\t\t   (s1w :after s1a))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :FullAff s1w)\n\t\t(AUTH :FullAff s1p)\n\t\t(s1p :PrtAff purpose)\n\t\t(purpose :FullNeg s1a)))\n```\n\nThe `:modstr` value represents any modals or\nnegation that are present within the purpose clause. That is, this value\ndoesn’t capture the fact that the purpose clause itself imparts a\nnon-full epistemic stance on its events; that is captured by the\n`:purpose` relation.\n\n[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n\n##### Part 4-3-1-5. condition relation\n\nThe `:condition` relation in the sentence-level annotation indicates the relationship between events in conditional constructions. These events\nalso receive a `:modstr` annotation. As can\nbe seen in <a href=\"#4-3-1-5 (1)\">(1)</a>, the event in the protasis is annotated with a `:condition` relation to the event in the\napodosis.\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-5 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-5 (1)\">4-3-1-5 (1)</span>\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-5 (1a)\" label=\"4-3-1-5 (1a)\">4-3-1-5 (1a)</span> \n```\nIf she’s hungry, I’ll feed her dinner.\n(f/ feed-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:ref-person 1st\n\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t:ARG1 (d/ dinner)\n\t:ARG2 (p2/ person\n\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t:condition (h/ hunger-01\n\t\t:ARG0 p2\n\t\t:aspect State\n\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n\t:aspect Performance\n\t:modstr FullAff)\n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((DCT :overlap s1h)\n\t\t   (DCT :after s1f))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :NeutAff have-condition)\n\t\t(have-condition :FullAff s1h)\n\t\t(have-condition :FullAff s1f)))\n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-5 (1b)\" label=\"4-3-1-5 (1b)\">4-3-1-5 (1b)</span> \n```\nIf she’s hungry, maybe I’ll cook pasta.\n(c/ cook-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:ref-person 1st\n\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t:ARG1 (p2/ pasta)\n\t:condition (h/ hunger-01\n\t\t:ARG0 (p3/ person\n\t\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t\t:aspect State\n\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n\t:aspect Performance\n\t:modstr NeutAff)\n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((DCT :overlap s1h)\n\t\t   (DCT :after s1c))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :NeutAff have-condition)\n\t\t(have-condition :FullAff s1h)\n\t\t(have-condition :NeutAff s1c)))\n```\n<span id=\"4-3-1-5 (1c)\" label=\"4-3-1-5 (1c)\">4-3-1-5 (1c)</span> \n```\nIf she isn’t hungry, we’ll just watch a movie.\n(w/ watch-01\n\t:ARG0 (p/ person\n\t\t:ref-person 1st\n\t\t:ref-number Plural)\n\t:ARG1 (m/ movie)\n\t:mod (j/ just)\n\t:condition (h/ hunger-01\n\t\t:ARG0 (p2/ person\n\t\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n\t\t\t:ref-number Singular)\n\t\t:aspect State\n\t\t:modstr FullNeg)\n\t:aspect Performance\n\t:modstr FullAff)\n(s/ sentence\n\t:temporal ((DCT :overlap s1h)\n\t\t   (DCT :after s1w))\n\t:modal ((AUTH :NeutAff have-condition)\n\t\t(have-condition :FullNeg s1h)\n\t\t(have-condition :FullAff s1c))) \n```\nAs with purpose clauses, the `:modstr` value\ndoesn’t capture the uncertainty imparted by the conditional construction\nitself; it corresponds to any negation or modals which are expressed\ninside of the conditional construction. The modal value of the\nconditional construction is captured by the\n`:condition` value.\n\n[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n\n##### Part 4-3-1-6. Modal dependency structure\n\nAt Stage 0, there are some pieces of the modal dependency structure that\nare unspecified. The modal strength that a modal verb imparts on its\ncomplement is one of these pieces. As the modal annotation progresses,\nthis information is added to the frame files for modal verbs. For\nexample, the complements of English *want* have a\n`:modstr NeutAff` value; this will be indicated\nin the frame file as shown below.\n\n```\nPredicate: want.01 \n\tRoles:  \n\tArg0: wanter  \n\tArg1: thing wanted  \n\tArg2: beneficiary  \n\tArg3: in-exchange-for  \n\tArg4: from \n\n:modstr of complement: NeutAff  \n```\n\nFor the complements of modals, the `:modstr`\nvalues work largely the same way that they do for other events; however,\nthey reflect the beliefs of the\n`:experiencer` participant of the modal\nevent, who is often not the author. For example, in\n<a href=\"#4-3-1-6 (1)\">(1)</a>, Mary believes that the visit event may take\nplace in the future (`NeutAff` strength), but\nthe author disagrees.\n\n<span id=\"4-3-1-6 (1)\" label=\"4-3-1-6 (1)\">\\[4-3-1-6 (1)\\]</span> Mary wants\nto visit France next month, but I don’t think that’s possible.\n\nThe value associated with the modal event in its frame file corresponds\nto Mary’s beliefs, as the `:experiencer` of\nthe wanting event.\n\nSome predicates impart full, positive\n(`FullAff`) strength on their complements,\noften called factive predicates (e.g., *manage to*). Strong epistemic\nmodals (e.g., *expect that, deduce*) and strong deontic modals,\nincluding intention modals (e.g., *plan to, decide to*) and obligation\nmodals (e.g., *need, demand*), impart `PrtAff`\nstrength on their complements. Weak deontic modals, including desire\n(e.g., *want*) and permission (e.g., *allow*), impart\n`NeutAff` strength on their complements.\nCertain modals may also lexicalize negation, such as *doubt*, *forbid*,\nor *wish*. These are annotated with the\n`NeutNeg`,\n`PrtNeg`, and\n`FullNeg` values, respectively.\n\n[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n\n#### Part 4-3-2. English modals\n\nThis list gives the modal strength value associated with common English\nmodal constructions (this is certaintly not an exhaustive list). For\nmodal predicates that are identified as their own event (e.g.,\ndeontic predicates), the modal strength value characterizes the dependency link between the modal predicate node and its child event. For example, *want* is in the `NeutAff` list, which indicates that there\nis a `NeutAff` link between the `want` node and its\ncomplement event node in the full dependency structure.   \n\n`FullAff` (full affirmative)\n\n  - Simple assertions: declarative sentences\n\n  - Certainty: *certainly, be sure, definitely, necessarily*\n\n  - Predictive future: *will,* non-intentional *be going to*","numLines":850,"startLine":7109,"totalLines":8722}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TrZH3WCXUUPXeC85zqA3qy","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
