{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TzDha3XwSF1nkeeswLKMsM","type":"tool_result","content":"700\tneeds to be standardized for computational tractability. This applies\n701\tto, for example, the annotation of different types of quantities (which\n702\tuse abstract concepts such as `distance-quantity`,\n703\t`temporal-quantity` and `monetary-quantity`, as\n704\tillustrated in <a href=\"#2-2-3 (2a)\">(2a)</a>-<a href=\"#2-2-3 (2b)\">(2b)</a>, and the annotation of dates and times (which use abstract concepts such as `date-entity`, as illustrated in <a href=\"#2-2-3 (2c)\">(2c)</a>, all from Sanapaná). More information on abstract concepts can be found throughout this document in the sections dedicated to the specific semantic phenomena for which they are needed.\n705\t\n706\t<span id=\"2-2-3 (2)\">2-2-3 (2)</span>\n707\t\n708\t<span id=\"2-2-3 (2a)\">2-2-3 (2a)</span>\n709\t```\n710\tcinco,\tseis\tmeses\tapk-ehl-ta'mehl-kes-kam-a\n711\tfive\tsix\tmonths\t2/3M-DSTR-be.good-CAUS-TI-NOM\n712\t'They would preserve it for five or six months.'\n713\t(e/ enta'mehlkeskama-00 'preserve'\n714\t\t:actor (p/ person\n715\t\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n716\t\t\t:ref-number Plural)\n717\t\t:undergoer (t/ thing)\n718\t\t:duration (o/ or\n719\t\t\t:op1 (t2/ temporal-quantity\n720\t\t\t\t:quant 5\n721\t\t\t\t:unit (m/ mes 'month'))\n722\t\t\t:op2 (t3/ temporal-quantity\n723\t\t\t\t:quant 6\n724\t\t\t\t:unit (m/ mes 'month')))\n725\t\t:aspect Habitual\n726\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n727\t```\n728\t<span id=\"2-2-3 (2b)\">2-2-3 (2b)</span>\n729\t```\n730\tescuela\tagrícola\tseis\tmillón\tcada\taño\ton-yanmong-kes-ek\n731\tschool\tagricultural\tsix\tmillion\teach\tyear\t1PL.IRR-exchange-CAUS-FUT\n732\t'For the agricultural school, we would pay six million (guaranies) per year.'\n733\t(e/ enyanmongkeskama-00 'pay'\n734\t\t:actor (p/ person\n735\t\t\t:ref-person 1st\n736\t\t\t:ref-number Plural)\n737\t\t:recipient (e2/ escuela 'school'\n738\t\t\t:mod (a/ agrícola 'agricultural'))\n739\t\t:theme (m/ monetary-quantity\n740\t\t\t:quant 6,000,000\n741\t\t\t:unit (g/ guaraní\n742\t\t\t\t:mod (c/ country\n743\t\t\t\t\t:wiki \"Paraguay\"\n744\t\t\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Paraguay\"))))\n745\t\t:frequency (r/ rate-entity\n746\t\t\t:ARG1 1\n747\t\t\t:ARG2 (t/ temporal-quantity\n748\t\t\t\t:quant 1\n749\t\t\t\t:unit (a2/ año 'year)))\n750\t\t:aspect Habitual\n751\t\t:modstr FullAff)\n752\t```\n753\t<span id=\"2-2-3 (2c)\">2-2-3 (2c)</span>\n754\t```\n755\tapk-el-v-ayk-akh-a'\t\tVenancio\tel-eyv-om-akha'\t\tmil\t\tnovecientos\tsesenta\ty\tseis=anehla\n756\t2/3M-DSTR-arrive-TI-DUPL-NOM\tVenancio\t1PL-live-TI-NOM.LOC\tthousand\tnine-hundred\tsixty\tand\tsix=DUB\n757\t'Venancio and his companions arrived where we lived in ninteen sixty-six, maybe.'\n758\t(e/ elvay'a-00 'arrive'\n759\t\t:actor (p/ person\n760\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Venancio\")\n761\t\t:companion (p2/ person))\n762\t\t:goal (p3/ place\n763\t\t\t:Place-of (e2/ eleyvoma-00 'live'\n764\t\t\t\t:actor (p4/ person\n765\t\t\t\t\t:ref-person 1st\n766\t\t\t\t\t:ref-number Plural)))\n767\t\t:temporal (d/ date-entity\n768\t\t\t:year 1966)\n769\t\t:aspect Performance\n770\t\t:modstr NeutAff)\n771\t```\n772\t[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n773\t\n774\t#### Part 2-2-4. Non-participant role relations\n775\t\n776\tAnother domain in which AMR conventions are inherited by UMR is the\n777\tannotation of non-participant role relations. Such relations are used to\n778\tannotate a wide range of meanings. Many of them, such as `:medium`,\n779\t`:topic`, and `:mod` (the latter of which is illustrated in <a href=\"#2-2-3 (2b)\">(2-2-3 2b)</a> are used to\n780\tannotate modifiers of referring expressions. Other relations are used\n781\twith specific abstract concepts: whenever a `date-entity`\n782\tconcept is present, one of the temporal relations `:month`, `:year`, `:day`\n783\tetc. will be used, for example. The use of these relations is treated\n784\tmore in detail in [Part 3-2-2](#part-3-2-2-Non-participant-role-UMR-relations) of this document.\n785\t\n786\tEven though many of these relations are straightforwardly inherited by\n787\tUMR, some changes are made to their use for the sake of cross-linguistic\n788\tcomparability. Firstly, UMR adjusts the use of the `:mod` relation to\n789\tcover all *typifying* modifiers - i.e. modifiers which subcategorize the\n790\treference of a referring expression rather than narrowing it down to a\n791\tspecific entity (e.g. *women* in *women's magazine*). In AMR, this\n792\tsemantic domain was shared between the `:mod` relation and a number of\n793\tother relations such as `:medium`, `:topic`, and `:group`. In UMR, these\n794\tother relations still exist, but they are treated as more fine-grained\n795\tvalues on a lattice underneath the more general `:mod` relation. Whereas\n796\tAMR used the `:frequency` and `:duration` relations as the main ways of\n797\tannotating aspect, UMR does this through a dedicated `:aspect` attribute.\n798\tHowever, UMR maintains the `:duration` and `:frequency` relations so that\n799\tannotators can use them to indicate more fine-grained aspectual\n800\tinformation beyond the aspect categories offered in the corresponding\n801\tattribute, as in <a href=\"#2-2-3 (2b)\">(2-2-3 2b)</a>. UMR also adds a new non-participant role relation: the `:other-role` relation, which annotators can use when encountering meanings that UMR currently does not provide a straightforward annotation procedure for.\n802\t\n803\t[Back to Table of Contents](#toc)\n804\t\n805\t#### Part 2-2-5. Attributes\n806\t\n807\tThe last area in which AMR conventions are inherited by UMR is that of\n808\t*attribute* annotations. Attributes are relations which attach one of a\n809\tclosed, pre-defined list of values to a concept node: for example, the"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b1f75d6-aaf3-4c40-8364-7ab0d9bdc500","uuid":"67af886d-df8f-4cc4-a5a6-06d093635fb4","timestamp":"2026-11-10T07:29:47.636Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/umr-harness/s1-full/zh-0032/guidelines/umr_guidelines.md","content":"needs to be standardized for computational tractability. This applies\nto, for example, the annotation of different types of quantities (which\nuse abstract concepts such as `distance-quantity`,\n`temporal-quantity` and `monetary-quantity`, as\nillustrated in <a href=\"#2-2-3 (2a)\">(2a)</a>-<a href=\"#2-2-3 (2b)\">(2b)</a>, and the annotation of dates and times (which use abstract concepts such as `date-entity`, as illustrated in <a href=\"#2-2-3 (2c)\">(2c)</a>, all from Sanapaná). More information on abstract concepts can be found throughout this document in the sections dedicated to the specific semantic phenomena for which they are needed.\n\n<span id=\"2-2-3 (2)\">2-2-3 (2)</span>\n\n<span id=\"2-2-3 (2a)\">2-2-3 (2a)</span>\n```\ncinco,\tseis\tmeses\tapk-ehl-ta'mehl-kes-kam-a\nfive\tsix\tmonths\t2/3M-DSTR-be.good-CAUS-TI-NOM\n'They would preserve it for five or six months.'\n(e/ enta'mehlkeskama-00 'preserve'\n\t:actor (p/ person\n\t\t:ref-person 3rd\n\t\t:ref-number Plural)\n\t:undergoer (t/ thing)\n\t:duration (o/ or\n\t\t:op1 (t2/ temporal-quantity\n\t\t\t:quant 5\n\t\t\t:unit (m/ mes 'month'))\n\t\t:op2 (t3/ temporal-quantity\n\t\t\t:quant 6\n\t\t\t:unit (m/ mes 'month')))\n\t:aspect Habitual\n\t:modstr FullAff)\n```\n<span id=\"2-2-3 (2b)\">2-2-3 (2b)</span>\n```\nescuela\tagrícola\tseis\tmillón\tcada\taño\ton-yanmong-kes-ek\nschool\tagricultural\tsix\tmillion\teach\tyear\t1PL.IRR-exchange-CAUS-FUT\n'For the agricultural school, we would pay six million (guaranies) per year.'\n(e/ enyanmongkeskama-00 'pay'\n\t:actor (p/ person\n\t\t:ref-person 1st\n\t\t:ref-number Plural)\n\t:recipient (e2/ escuela 'school'\n\t\t:mod (a/ agrícola 'agricultural'))\n\t:theme (m/ monetary-quantity\n\t\t:quant 6,000,000\n\t\t:unit (g/ guaraní\n\t\t\t:mod (c/ country\n\t\t\t\t:wiki \"Paraguay\"\n\t\t\t\t:name (n/ name :op1 \"Paraguay\"))))\n\t:frequency (r/ rate-